import json,sys
out=sys.argv[1]
OVN="Share this with your players before they build characters."
INN="A suggested opening scene; adapt it or write your own."
MEN="Rules that apply only to this campaign frame."
an={
"name":"Dark Heart of Andaluria",
"pages":{"overview":"p. 145","incident":"p. 151","mechanics":"pp. 152–157"},
"overviewNote":OVN,
"overview":[
"Andaluria is a hot, volcanic wine country that won independence from Emperor Arcedrio in a revolt a hundred years ago. It is now run by the Council of Five and grows rich on vineyards and harbor trade, but old rituals, secret deals and hungry monsters still shape life there.",
"The dormant Serracorte volcanoes in the north shelter vineyards and shepherd country. The Valmorria River runs south through the valleys to the coastal capital, Jesaña, famous for its summer music, dancing and drinking.",
"Monsters such as ghosts and ghouls have always preyed on Andalurians. For generations, people kept them at bay with the Church of the Golden Eminence's rites and the folk magic of the nomadic Caio. Those protections are now failing.",
"This year, each night stretches longer and grows deadlier as the solstice approaches. The moon hangs in the sky too long, monsters roam the fields, and the rich hide at masked balls in the hilltop castillo of Condesa Ysabela Delamoña IV. People fear the coming Midsummer Festival will end in bloodshed. Meanwhile, Archbishop Veyra wins followers by preaching a purified faith that condemns Caio \"heresy.\"",
"Ordinary Andalurians have started hunting monsters themselves, and secret cells of the Sombral Spade, led by the mysterious Julio Cantafierro, fight back with steel, fire and forbidden lore. The common people cheer them, the church calls them heretics, and the powerful put up with them because they need them. The PCs are one such band of hunters, and they risk becoming monsters themselves."
],
"pullQuote":"When the festival wine runs red, someone has to hunt what crawled out of the cask.",
"incidentNote":INN,
"incident":[
"Setup: At Santavio's wine festival, the first barrels of Condesa Delamoña's prized vintage are opened and give off a foul reek. Monsters made of vine, claw and bloody pulp burst out and drag villagers off into the vineyards.",
"The Council of Five sends the Nightwatch under Andrea Nocta. Their real orders are to protect the remaining casks, not the villagers, and they are not trained to fight monsters if the creatures come back.",
"The villagers barricade themselves indoors and argue over the cause. Some say the Condesa waters her vines with innocent blood and the vengeful dead now live in the grapes. Archbishop Mateo Veyra blames Caio heretics and calls the frightened villagers into his church.",
"Julio Cantafierro of the Sombral Spade asks the party to track the creatures, rescue anyone still alive, and destroy whatever is corrupting the vineyard so this can't happen again.",
"Open questions: What ties the party to Cantafierro and the Spade, and what drives them: pay, honor or something else? Will they go after the people who were taken or protect the ones left behind? What detail in witness accounts frightens them most, and what trail did the monsters leave?"
],
"keyFigures":[
{"name":"Julio Cantafierro","role":"Sombral Spade leader","line":"Hires the party to hunt the vineyard horrors."},
{"name":"Condesa Ysabela Delamoña IV","role":"Reclusive noble vintner","line":"Her cursed wine unleashed the monsters; rumors swirl about her."},
{"name":"Archbishop Mateo Veyra","role":"Zealous church leader","line":"Blames the Caio and exploits the panic to gain followers."}
],
"start":"The village of Santavio, the night of its wine festival",
"mechanicsNote":MEN,
"mechanicsSub":"Monster-Hunting Equipment",
"mechanics":[
{"head":"Monster-Hunting Equipment","t":"Encourage players to reskin standard gear to fit the gothic monster-hunter style. The frame also adds new options that can be picked at character creation or handed out as loot:\n\nPrimary weapons: Blessed Brass Knuckles, Holy Shotgun, Repeating Crossbow.\nSecondary weapons: Wooden Stake, Hallowed Shield, Chain Whip.\nArmor: Coffinwood Armor, Leather Longcoat, Silverweave Armor.\n\nEach weapon's damage scales by tier, and each armor's thresholds and score scale by tier. They carry features such as hitting everything in front of you, adding a quick extra target, bonus damage when paired, extra Hope on a crit, pulling targets into Melee, bonus thresholds from unmarked Armor Slots, quieter movement, and reduced magic damage. Full tables are on pp. 152–153."},
{"head":"Transformations: Reanimated","t":"If a PC dies, ask the player whether they'd like the character to come back reanimated. If so, decide together between sessions where they wake up (a hidden lab, workshop or place of power). They gain the Reanimated transformation and their maximum loadout drops to one. Each rest, they can spend a downtime move to raise it by one, back up to the usual five."},
{"head":"Transformations: Vampire","t":"Give a major vampire adversary the Vampire's Curse action. On a successful Melee attack, the GM can spend a Fear to bite the target, dealing physical damage of a number of d8s equal to the vampire's tier, and then rolls a d6. If the d6 matches any of the damage dice, the target marks all their Stress and becomes a Vampire. If a player wants their PC turned, the attack simply succeeds."},
{"head":"Transformations: Werewolf","t":"Give a major werewolf adversary the Wolf's Curse action. On a successful Melee attack, the GM can spend a Fear to bite, dealing physical damage of a number of d20s equal to the werewolf's tier. If the bite deals Major damage or more, the target starts a long-term Lycanthropy Countdown (6). It ticks down at each rest where the PC doesn't spend a downtime move to slow the curse, and when it triggers the PC becomes a Werewolf. A willing player's PC is hit automatically and starts the countdown no matter how much damage they took."},
{"head":"Implementing & Removing Transformations","t":"Check with the group before using transformations at all. If you leave them out, explain in the fiction why the PCs are immune, for example through charms, tattoos, tonics brewed in downtime, or an inherited resistance. If you use them, plan ways a PC could undo the change, such as a faction's ritual, a rare remedy in a distant village, or a known healer of the cursed. Player consent and agency come first."},
{"head":"The Hunt","t":"An optional framework for building a monster session or short arc, and it works for any major villain. It has five beats:\n\n• Arrival: a strange event pulls the PCs in.\n• Investigation: they follow leads and collect clues.\n• Escalation: they pull their findings together and make a plan.\n• Confrontation: they banish, bind or kill the monster.\n• Epilogue: loose ends are tied up.\n\nUse whatever parts help your table."},
{"head":"Making a Monster","t":"Monsters in this frame are tainted by the hellgate beneath the Serracorte and want to destroy, devour or corrupt Andalurians. Build one by answering four questions:\n\n• What is it? A folklore creature, an unusual cryptid, a corrupted human, or something new. Give it a twist.\n• What does it want? Pick a drive such as wrath, lust, power, sorrow, revenge, greed, hunger, envy or fear.\n• Where can you find it? A lair or hunting ground that makes a good set piece.\n• How can you defeat it? Every monster has a weakness: a material, a ritual, a circumstance, or a combination. Killed any other way, it escapes or comes back stronger.\n\nOptionally, add complications such as minions, vulnerable bystanders, and key events or places that show off the monster."},
{"head":"Preparing Liminal Clues","t":"Before play, answer the four monster questions, then write 4–6 Liminal Clues: pieces of evidence that each point toward an answer. Keep them on cards. They aren't tied to any location; one appears whenever the PCs investigate or take a risk somewhere it would fit. Once a clue is revealed, it becomes fixed fact. Mix broad clues that could turn up almost anywhere with specific ones that suit particular scenes.\n\nIt takes practice. Players may need to see the same information several ways before acting on it, and it's fine to repeat or reword a clue. When every clue has been found, the PCs should be ready to confront the monster."},
{"head":"Structure of a Hunt","t":"Guidance for each beat:\n\nArrival: an opening scene that hooks the PCs with something uncanny.\nInvestigation: the PCs follow leads, and clues come out through evidence, witnesses, experts, rumors, research or magic.\nEscalation: the PCs connect what they know and plan. If they stall, have the monster or its allies come after them or someone they care about.\nConfrontation: the PCs use the monster's weakness against it, usually in a fight.\nEpilogue: tie up threads, push long-term projects forward, and hint at the next mystery."},
{"head":"Example of Play","t":"A worked example (pp. 156–157) follows a GM building a ghost story: a drowned girl's spirit draining villagers in their sleep, with an old well as the setting, a list of Liminal Clues, and a planned outline for each beat. It then shows the session as played, including how the players' conclusions changed both the monster's lair and its weakness from the GM's plan, and why the GM went along with them."}
]}

ho={
"name":"Journey to Horizon",
"pages":{"overview":"p. 159","incident":"p. 168","mechanics":"pp. 169–179"},
"overviewNote":OVN,
"overview":[
"The goddess Aurelia raised the continent of Valloren from the sea and shaped wondrous landscapes using terrana, a renewable magical essence. She chose thirteen magi to watch over it, and an Age of Wonder followed.",
"Two of the magi, the Lovers Yrska and Drago, used terrana recklessly to build ever-grander cities and rebelled when Aurelia told them to stop. They won the other magi over one by one. With terrana nearly exhausted and her side cornered on the eastern coast, Aurelia sacrificed herself: she sealed her shrines behind barriers and brought down the stars, wiping out all thirteen traitors. As she died, she promised to return.",
"That catastrophe, the Starfall, devastated the land. For a thousand years—the Long Sorrow—survivors lived sealed inside isolated sanctuaries. When the barriers finally opened, they found New Valloren: wild, transformed by surging terrana, and poisoned by shadowblight seeping from the craters where stars fell, which spawns monsters.",
"High Priestess Saraya of the Marrogate sanctuary found a Soul Echo, a fragment of Aurelia's essence. She learned that collecting the Echoes scattered across Valloren and carrying them to the place Aurelia died could bring her back, and she made a Vessel of Light to hold them.",
"Enemies are moving. The Cult of the Two-Headed Coin, which worships the Lovers, has planted Fellsworn Templars inside Saraya's order, and shadowy Seekers-in-Shadow are appearing everywhere. When the Templars strike, Saraya hands the Vessel to the PCs and gives her life to cover their escape. Now they must cross a hostile continent, resting only in sanctuaries, to revive the goddess."
],
"pullQuote":"Carry the goddess home, one hex at a time.",
"incidentNote":"A suggested opening scene; adapt it or write your own. Adjust the Soul Echo and Vessel to match your table's descriptions.",
"incident":[
"Setup: Soon after the sanctuary walls fell, Saraya Rinn, a high priestess of the Aurelian Order, found Aurelia's Soul Echo at Marrogate's central shrine. She spent weeks making the Vessel of Light, then planned the trip to the Cliffs of Horizon with her apprentice Tolren and chose local citizens to guard it.",
"On departure day, armed priests surround her and Tolren reveals he is a Fellsworn Templar loyal to the Lovers. None of her messages went out and no allies are waiting; he only tipped off the cult so they could take the Vessel.",
"Saraya calls on terrana to make plants erupt through the shrine floor, tangling the attackers and bringing the building down. In the confusion, she gives the Vessel to the party, makes them swear to bring it to Horizon, and tells them to find Abbot Kensington in nearby Cinteri.",
"Open questions: How will the party slip past the Templars searching the sanctuary? Who can they trust to help them escape? Once outside, will they go straight to Cinteri or take a riskier route to throw off pursuit?"
],
"keyFigures":[
{"name":"Saraya Rinn","role":"Aurelian high priestess","line":"Discovered the Echo and sacrifices herself to save the Vessel."},
{"name":"Tolren","role":"Traitorous apprentice, Fellsworn Templar","line":"Betrays Saraya to the Lovers' cult."},
{"name":"Abbot Kensington","role":"Potential ally in Cinteri","line":"The contact Saraya sends the party to find."}
],
"start":"The shrine of Marrogate sanctuary, at Valloren's southwestern tip",
"mechanicsNote":MEN,
"mechanicsSub":"The Hex Crawl",
"mechanics":[
{"head":"The Hex Crawl","t":"Travel across New Valloren uses a hex map, with each hex roughly 24 miles across (adjust as the story needs). The party moves between sanctuaries one hex at a time and deals with encounters along the way. In the wilderness they can take up to three short rests but no long rests, so they need to manage resources carefully.\n\nThe procedure exists to support the story, not replace it: long stretches of travel go by in montage, with a few scenes played out in detail. Bring players in by asking about rumors, strange sounds in the night, people they know nearby, or old legends of the region. Change or drop rules whenever the story calls for it."},
{"head":"Journeys in Valloren","t":"The goal is to find nine shrines holding Aurelia's remaining Soul Echoes, add each to the Vessel, and bring the Vessel to the Cliffs of Horizon. Use two copies of the map. The GM's key map records each hex's habitat, terrain, and points of interest or encounters. The players' map shows only sanctuaries and pre-Sorrow geography, and the players fill it in as they explore."},
{"head":"Preparing the GM's Key Map","t":"Fill in hexes by choosing, rolling, or a mix of both. You only need to prepare the areas the party will probably reach in the next session or two.\n\nPlacing Soul Echoes: before play, choose nine sanctuaries to hold Echoes. Spread them across the continent without an obvious pattern, and roughly from west to east, so early Echoes come quickly and the last one is near Horizon.\n\nFilling wilderness hexes, one region at a time: roll d20 for habitat, d12 for size in contiguous hexes (don't enclose empty hexes), d8+d6 for a signature encounter, d4 for terrain, which sets the days needed to enter each hex, and d100 for a rumor whose truth you decide."},
{"head":"Example Region Generation","t":"A worked example (p. 170): a GM rolls a small forest region with extreme terrain, a Cult of the Two-Headed Coin encounter, and a rumor about a half-buried clockwork machine. He places the region across the party's most direct route, makes the machine an Age of Wonder relic the cult is after, and plans the fight with the Battle Point system."},
{"head":"Habitat (d20)","t":"This table sets a region's main habitat: 1 Shadowblighted (roll again for the corrupted habitat; a second 1 means it's almost impassable), 2 Underground, 3–4 Aquatic, 5–6 Wetland, 7–8 Grassland, 9–10 Tropical, 11–12 Forest, 13–14 Drylands, 15–16 Rolling, 17–18 Mountain, 19 Frozen, 20 Badlands. Each entry lists example biomes. Because of terrana, very different biomes can sit side by side, and ordinary lakes and rivers don't need to be tracked. See p. 170."},
{"head":"Encounter (d8 + d6)","t":"This table gives a 2–14 bell curve of encounter types meant to keep the wilderness dangerous. Results include rolling twice and combining, other travelers, setbacks (optionally the GM gains a Fear per PC), Seekers-in-Shadow, extreme weather, hostile people, a beast's territory, environmental hazards, the Cult, a remarkable site, good luck (optionally each PC gains a Hope), a settlement, and hidden loot. Use them as starting points. See p. 171."},
{"head":"Terrain (d4)","t":"Terrain sets how many days it takes to enter a hex and is usually the same across a region: 1 Optimal (roads; about 1 day; more fellow travelers), 2 Fair (trails; 2 days), 3 Rough (game tracks and undergrowth; 3 days), 4 Extreme (cliffs, bogs, trackless wilds; 4 days, and may need gear or magic). See p. 171."},
{"head":"Rumors (d100)","t":"A table of 100 short, strange region hooks. You decide whether each is true, false or partly true, whether the party has heard it, and how they heard it, such as at a sanctuary, from a merchant, or from signs on the road. Full table on pp. 172–173."},
{"head":"Dice Tables for Creating Sanctuaries","t":"Generate a sanctuary by rolling d20 Trade & Exports, d12 Quirk (customs, art, taboos and the like), d10 Crisis, d10 Drive, d8 Political System, d6 Size (isolated dwelling up to city), and d4 Population (sparse to crowded). Then weave the results into one story. The worked example turns the rolls into a declining port town known for terrana liquors and painted murals, run by a corrupt merchant guild and trying to rebuild its reputation. Build each sanctuary before the session where the party will arrive. Tables on p. 174."},
{"head":"Settlement Name (d100×2)","t":"Roll d100 twice on a table of name parts and combine one element from each roll to name a settlement, sanctuary or landmark. Table on p. 175."},
{"head":"Tracking the Party's Location","t":"The players mark their progress on their own map. The PCs are skilled pathfinders, so they know what's in their current hex and the adjacent ones, including any visible landmarks there. The GM decides how much else to tell them."},
{"head":"Travel Days","t":"A normal travel day is: break camp before dawn, travel about 6–8 hours, stop at midday, travel another 6–8 hours until dusk, then make camp and set a watch. Scouting, foraging, repairs and keeping watch happen in the background. Play them out only if the table enjoys it."},
{"head":"Resting","t":"There is no ration tracking; rests stand in for supplies. In the wilderness, a short rest means spending a whole day in one place. A long rest takes several days and is only possible inside a sanctuary. Sleeping at night is fiction only and gives no mechanical rest. After three short rests in a row, the next rest must be a long rest (core p. 105), so the party has to head back to civilization to recover fully.\n\nSpecial downtime move, Locate Soul Echoes: a PC communes with the Vessel and learns the general compass direction of the nearest sanctuary holding an Echo. At the GM's discretion, this can also alert nearby Seekers-in-Shadow to the Vessel's general direction."},
{"head":"Encounter Rolls","t":"When the party enters a hex, roll d6s equal to its terrain rating. Any 1 triggers the region's prepared encounter; otherwise the GM can spend a Fear to trigger one anyway. Use d4s for more dangerous areas or d8s for safer ones. If a region triggers more than once, roll a new encounter or borrow and reskin one from a region the party is skipping. A worked example is on p. 176.\n\nOptional Endurance Countdowns: after a rest, each PC starts an Endurance Countdown (6). On entering a wilderness hex, each player rolls their Hope Die: a result at or below the countdown's current value means they mark a Stress; otherwise the countdown ticks down. When it triggers, the PC is Vulnerable until their next rest. All countdowns end when a rest begins.\n\nWhen disaster looms: if the party is stranded with no short rests and every HP and Stress marked, the GM chooses the outcome. Options include the quest failing, a new party taking up the Vessel (possibly years later), or a rescue or capture that costs them the Vessel and sets up a mission to get it back."},
{"head":"Traveling over Water","t":"Water travel uses the overland rules with a few changes. Rivers: with a boat and a navigable waterway, going downstream lowers a hex's terrain rating by 1, and going upstream adds a day. Ocean: with a ship, roll a d4 for weather on entering each hex, and the result is the number of days it takes (1 tailwind, 2 fair, 3 rough water, 4 extreme weather). Describe how hard the crew work wears the PCs down."},
{"head":"Character Advancement","t":"The party levels up each time they add a Soul Echo to the Vessel. They start with one Echo and collect nine more, which takes them from level 1 to level 10."},
{"head":"Doom Track","t":"The Doom Track (in the appendix, or downloadable) tracks disasters spreading across New Valloren. At the end of each session, mark one box at or below the party's tier; a nested box can only be marked after the box it hangs from. Work each marked event into play when it makes sense, at least once and possibly more. Skip a mark when the heroes push back the darkness, or add extra marks when they dawdle or let evil spread, such as a sanctuary falling to the Cult. It's paced for about 30–40 sessions, so adjust for a shorter or longer campaign."},
{"head":"Campaign Adversaries","t":"There are two features you can add to any adversary. Shadowblighted (Passive): the creature is visibly corrupted, and in or near shadowblight it crits on 18–20. Terranamancer (Passive): the adversary marks a Stress to use a spell tied to the local habitat. Options include Heat, Overgrowth, Wind, Ice (inflicts Frostbitten, −1 Proficiency), Poison (inflicts Nauseated, no Hope gain), Stone (threshold bonus) and Water. Damage dice scale with the adversary's tier. Details on p. 178."},
{"head":"Full Gameplay Example","t":"A detailed two-page walkthrough (pp. 178–179) follows a party leaving one sanctuary for another 10 hexes away that may hold an Echo. It shows route choices between terrain types, encounter rolls, rests and Endurance Countdowns in action, with map illustrations that use fog of war."}
]}
json.dump(an,open(out+"/andaluria.json","w"),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)
json.dump(ho,open(out+"/horizon.json","w"),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)
